# "passion" might look like it shifts between "justice can be without passions" and "the more perfect the virtue, the more it causes passion".
# But those are different claims, and each claim keeps one sense in all the statements that use it.
# The text itself reconciles them: the joy is not a passion in the will, and becomes a passion only when it overflows into the sensitive appetite.
# So no statement acts as a bridge between two senses.
